00:00:25.850 --> 00:00:27.830
PROFESSOR: So this
is a really, really,

00:00:27.830 --> 00:00:31.720
really, really stunning
fact in a sense of we

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:38.260
talk about lots of forms of
inequality and unfairness

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:38.830
in the world.

00:00:38.830 --> 00:00:45.700
But here's a fact that's really
quite, quite, quite striking.

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:48.895
You start from the
basic, just the fact.

00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:56.200
It's the fact that if you look
at the ratio of men and women

00:00:56.200 --> 00:01:02.190
in the population, most
countries in the world

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:05.080
have many more women than men.

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:11.836
This is mostly because women
are just better designed,

00:01:11.836 --> 00:01:14.460
it turns out.

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:18.990
When men get to the
age of about 60,

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:21.520
various bad things
happen to them.

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:26.410
In particular, their
arteries seem to harden.

00:01:26.410 --> 00:01:30.500
And lots of them die from
various cardiovascular

00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:31.460
diseases.

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:38.390
So as a result, most countries
have many more women than men.

00:01:38.390 --> 00:01:45.020
So the ratios up
there, Europe has 1.05.

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:54.190
Sub-Saharan Africa has 1.02.

00:01:54.190 --> 00:01:55.900
All of Latin America is above 1.

00:01:55.900 --> 00:01:57.490
The US is about 1.

00:01:57.490 --> 00:02:03.130
And while it doesn't look like a
lot, 4%, we're talking about 4%

00:02:03.130 --> 00:02:08.310
of 3 billion.

00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:12.570
So that's a fair number of
extra women we're talking about.

00:02:12.570 --> 00:02:15.620
The world has many more
women than men overall.

00:02:15.620 --> 00:02:18.320
But the one place
where that's not true

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:24.230
is in this belt which goes
from basically North Africa

00:02:24.230 --> 00:02:25.780
to Korea.

00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:30.140
That's that one belt in the
world pretty much all the way.

00:02:30.140 --> 00:02:37.290
You can draw a belt starting
somewhere in Morocco

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:41.820
and keep going east,
about at that latitude.

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:43.920
And then go a bit
north into China,

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:45.680
and you'll find
that that's where

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:49.460
they are about 4% or
less women than men.

00:03:04.565 --> 00:03:06.690
These numbers are not
necessarily the worst numbers

00:03:06.690 --> 00:03:07.523
you're going to see.

00:03:07.523 --> 00:03:11.300
So I'll show you some more
striking numbers in a minute.

00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:15.570
I mean, just the fact
that there's 6% less women

00:03:15.570 --> 00:03:24.800
than men is striking,
given that that doesn't

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:26.330
look like the biological model.

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:36.720
So Amartya Sen, who was a
Nobel Prize winner at Harvard

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:40.550
did this calculation, basically
very simple calculation.

00:03:40.550 --> 00:03:43.540
He said, look,
Sub-Saharan Africa

00:03:43.540 --> 00:03:45.080
is the poorest
part of the world.

00:03:49.470 --> 00:03:54.680
So if there is some maybe
institutional reason why women

00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:56.720
die, so there might be
reasons why women die

00:03:56.720 --> 00:03:58.010
that have to do with poverty.

00:03:58.010 --> 00:04:01.480
What's an example of a
reason, which would hurt women

00:04:01.480 --> 00:04:05.320
more than men,
reason why poverty

00:04:05.320 --> 00:04:07.816
would hurt women more than men?

00:04:07.816 --> 00:04:09.052
AUDIENCE: During childbirth.

00:04:09.052 --> 00:04:11.260
PROFESSOR: Childbirth, the
obvious one is childbirth.

00:04:11.260 --> 00:04:11.760
Yeah?

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:13.710
AUDIENCE: And then
compounding upon that,

00:04:13.710 --> 00:04:18.477
if women in poor situations have
less access to contraceptives

00:04:18.477 --> 00:04:21.060
and have more children, which
increases the lack [INAUDIBLE]..

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:22.935
There will be complications
for [INAUDIBLE]..

00:04:22.935 --> 00:04:25.010
PROFESSOR: So everything
to do with childbirth

00:04:25.010 --> 00:04:30.310
is reason why you might
think that in poor countries,

00:04:30.310 --> 00:04:34.990
women will have a disadvantage.

00:04:34.990 --> 00:04:37.360
You do see evidence
of the disadvantage.

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:41.250
Sub-Saharan Africa has
less women than Europe.

00:04:41.250 --> 00:04:45.320
So you do see an example
of that disadvantage.

00:04:45.320 --> 00:04:48.340
It's about 2 percentage
points or 3 percentage points

00:04:48.340 --> 00:04:55.320
less women than Europe.

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:58.380
If you think of Sub-Saharan
Africa as the part of the world

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:01.860
which has the highest
maternal mortality rates.

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:04.680
It's the part of the world
which has the highest fertility

00:05:04.680 --> 00:05:05.180
rates.

00:05:05.180 --> 00:05:08.070
Both to your point
and your point,

00:05:08.070 --> 00:05:10.630
if you take the part of
the world where women

00:05:10.630 --> 00:05:12.770
are most likely to
die in childbirth,

00:05:12.770 --> 00:05:18.460
and the place where women
have the most children,

00:05:18.460 --> 00:05:20.462
both of those are highest
in Sub-Saharan Africa.

00:05:20.462 --> 00:05:21.920
So you could think
of this as being

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:25.350
a reasonable approximation
of the worst case scenario.

00:05:25.350 --> 00:05:28.850
Take the worst case scenario
and then go to China.

00:05:28.850 --> 00:05:30.270
So that's the exercise.

00:05:30.270 --> 00:05:33.570
Let's take the
worst case scenario.

00:05:33.570 --> 00:05:39.580
Now go to China and
ask, if we applied

00:05:39.580 --> 00:05:43.900
the Sub-Saharan African ratio
to the Chinese population,

00:05:43.900 --> 00:05:46.370
how many more women
would they have had?

00:05:46.370 --> 00:05:49.450
The answer is they would have
had 44 million more women.

00:05:49.450 --> 00:05:53.420
So 44 million more
women are missing.

00:05:53.420 --> 00:05:59.760
And you keep doing that,
and same order of magnitude

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:03.640
in India, and then smaller
countries, so smaller numbers,

00:06:03.640 --> 00:06:05.850
but it all adds up
toward 100 million.

00:06:05.850 --> 00:06:09.760
So you get about 100 million
missing women in the world

00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:12.300
by doing this simple exercise.

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:18.690
And that's sort of
prima facie evidence.

00:06:18.690 --> 00:06:21.690
As we'll see, you have to
think harder about exactly

00:06:21.690 --> 00:06:22.950
what this evidence means.

00:06:22.950 --> 00:06:25.420
This is prima facie evidence
of something very bizarre

00:06:25.420 --> 00:06:25.920
happening.

00:06:29.450 --> 00:06:31.330
We'll see what's going on here.

00:06:31.330 --> 00:06:34.150
But there's something very
troubling about this fact.

00:06:34.150 --> 00:06:36.940
Because it seems like
somehow populations

00:06:36.940 --> 00:06:40.200
are managing to
get rid of women,

00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:44.552
quote unquote, "losing
women" through mechanisms

00:06:44.552 --> 00:06:46.510
which are not the normal
biological mechanisms.

00:06:50.090 --> 00:06:52.520
So that's the missing women.

00:06:52.520 --> 00:06:53.020
Yeah?

00:06:53.020 --> 00:06:55.882
AUDIENCE: So this order
for killing women in China,

00:06:55.882 --> 00:06:57.790
are they missing in rural China?

00:06:57.790 --> 00:06:59.383
Or is it just all around China?

00:06:59.383 --> 00:07:00.925
PROFESSOR: I'll show
you in a minute.

00:07:00.925 --> 00:07:03.300
I'll show you all kinds of
facts about China, in fact.

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:11.140
So before I come
to that, there's

00:07:11.140 --> 00:07:14.970
just some hypotheses
you might have.

00:07:14.970 --> 00:07:21.370
One is, is development
the answer?

00:07:21.370 --> 00:07:30.620
Maybe there's some
reason why just

00:07:30.620 --> 00:07:35.510
being undeveloped for reasons
we don't understand translates

00:07:35.510 --> 00:07:36.630
into less women.

00:07:36.630 --> 00:07:41.800
Maybe we have a theory
that maybe it's the case

00:07:41.800 --> 00:07:44.830
that people are more
traditional, something.

00:07:44.830 --> 00:07:47.790
Now what makes this
fact much more worrying

00:07:47.790 --> 00:07:51.870
is that development is not
making this fact go away.

00:07:51.870 --> 00:07:58.940
That's sort of at least
within modern history.

00:07:58.940 --> 00:08:02.320
We don't know what
happened 100 years ago.

00:08:02.320 --> 00:08:07.380
But right now, development is
not making this fact go away.

00:08:07.380 --> 00:08:11.090
If you look at the sex ratio
in India, it's not changing.

00:08:11.090 --> 00:08:15.250
It's in fact pretty much steady.

00:08:15.250 --> 00:08:16.770
I'll show you
numbers in a minute.

00:08:16.770 --> 00:08:20.950
If you look at the sex ratio
in China, it's falling.

00:08:20.950 --> 00:08:24.740
It's less and less women
every year in China.

00:08:24.740 --> 00:08:28.960
And if you look at the
richest parts of China

00:08:28.960 --> 00:08:31.890
versus the poorest
part to your question,

00:08:31.890 --> 00:08:34.260
you see a negative correlation.

00:08:34.260 --> 00:08:36.270
The riches parts of
China are the ones

00:08:36.270 --> 00:08:38.010
which have the least women.

00:08:38.010 --> 00:08:40.066
Yeah?

00:08:40.066 --> 00:08:42.561
AUDIENCE: Do we know
anything about missing men?

00:08:42.561 --> 00:08:45.555
Maybe there's some
component where there's just

00:08:45.555 --> 00:08:47.923
a general [INAUDIBLE]
for children

00:08:47.923 --> 00:08:49.048
in some parts of the world.

00:08:49.048 --> 00:08:50.545
And the number of
children in total

00:08:50.545 --> 00:08:54.510
are missing, not just the girls.

00:08:54.510 --> 00:08:56.650
PROFESSOR: That's
a good question.

00:08:56.650 --> 00:08:59.810
I think that's what
people do when they say,

00:08:59.810 --> 00:09:03.290
the infant mortality rate
is too high or something.

00:09:03.290 --> 00:09:08.653
And I think that
that's also a concern.

00:09:08.653 --> 00:09:11.070
It's not that there aren't
other concerns because of this.

00:09:16.170 --> 00:09:18.890
A lot of people would say
that you shouldn't just

00:09:18.890 --> 00:09:21.230
look at GDP to measure welfare.

00:09:21.230 --> 00:09:22.880
Emphasize infant
mortality rates.

00:09:22.880 --> 00:09:24.670
Infant mortality
rates is exactly

00:09:24.670 --> 00:09:27.570
the question you're asking,
missing boys and girls.

00:09:30.320 --> 00:09:33.900
That number is very high
in Sub-Saharan Africa.

00:09:33.900 --> 00:09:36.760
Sub-Saharan Africa has the
highest infant mortality

00:09:36.760 --> 00:09:39.040
rate in the world by far.

00:09:39.040 --> 00:09:41.710
China has now infant
mortality rates which

00:09:41.710 --> 00:09:45.410
are approaching that in the US.

00:09:45.410 --> 00:09:51.950
So infant morality rates
by itself, by now it's

00:09:51.950 --> 00:09:53.770
not a huge problem in China.

00:09:53.770 --> 00:09:57.800
China is down to
the range where it's

00:09:57.800 --> 00:09:59.760
very, very close to the US.

00:09:59.760 --> 00:10:03.470
I think it has infant mortality
rates of like 25 our of 1,000,

00:10:03.470 --> 00:10:04.890
1,000 live births.

00:10:04.890 --> 00:10:07.660
US has 18 or 14 or something.

00:10:07.660 --> 00:10:12.900
It's really in the range, where
Sub-Saharan Africa, Sierra

00:10:12.900 --> 00:10:17.520
Leone has 140.

00:10:17.520 --> 00:10:20.260
So if you want to see where
infant mortality is a problem,

00:10:20.260 --> 00:10:22.655
that's not where missing
women is a problem.

00:10:22.655 --> 00:10:24.270
These are different problems.

00:10:24.270 --> 00:10:25.782
It's not to say
you're not right.

00:10:25.782 --> 00:10:26.740
That's another problem.

00:10:26.740 --> 00:10:28.190
But it's a different one.

00:10:31.410 --> 00:10:39.300
So this is sort of just
to show you that fact.

00:10:47.180 --> 00:10:50.570
So what I showed you
so far was the levels,

00:10:50.570 --> 00:10:53.830
how many women per men.

00:10:53.830 --> 00:11:00.170
This is, in 2005,
what's the future.

00:11:00.170 --> 00:11:04.920
Future's much more
scary than the present.

00:11:04.920 --> 00:11:05.950
The present is what--

00:11:10.710 --> 00:11:13.440
there was like 95
girls per 100 boys.

00:11:13.440 --> 00:11:17.440
So that's about 105
boys per 100 girls.

00:11:17.440 --> 00:11:18.990
That's the current.

00:11:18.990 --> 00:11:22.555
The future, look at the
number for Eastern Asia.

00:11:25.220 --> 00:11:33.680
That number is 120
boys for 100 girls.

00:11:33.680 --> 00:11:36.980
It's a completely different
order of magnitude.

00:11:36.980 --> 00:11:38.590
Think of the future.

00:11:38.590 --> 00:11:41.942
These are the kids who
are being born under five.

00:11:41.942 --> 00:11:45.590
So at point, if this
is what stays steady,

00:11:45.590 --> 00:11:51.830
we are going to see numbers
more like 120 than 105.

00:11:51.830 --> 00:11:56.640
Right now there are 105
boys to 100 girls in China.

00:11:56.640 --> 00:12:00.600
And that number is going
to go up to closer to 120

00:12:00.600 --> 00:12:04.510
as this cohort ages.

00:12:04.510 --> 00:12:09.040
So that's just backing up
the first claim I made,

00:12:09.040 --> 00:12:10.880
which is it's not
getting better.

00:12:10.880 --> 00:12:14.230
If you look at the number
for India, it's 108.

00:12:14.230 --> 00:12:23.630
108 is bigger than the current
ratio of men to women in India.

00:12:23.630 --> 00:12:27.155
So current ratio is about
106, and that's 108.

00:12:27.155 --> 00:12:31.680
So all of these countries, which
are getting richer, their ratio

00:12:31.680 --> 00:12:35.235
is getting worse.

00:12:35.235 --> 00:12:36.860
Do you see what this
is, how it this is

00:12:36.860 --> 00:12:38.652
different from the one
I showed you before?

00:12:41.480 --> 00:12:44.320
This is the ratio
among under fives.

00:12:44.320 --> 00:12:47.360
So these are the future
of the population,

00:12:47.360 --> 00:12:50.200
whereas I showed you the
present of the population.

00:12:50.200 --> 00:12:51.450
Future's worse, not better.

00:12:57.880 --> 00:13:01.725
Korea has the same kind of
numbers, which are worse.

00:13:05.970 --> 00:13:09.790
Interestingly,
Afghanistan and Nepal,

00:13:09.790 --> 00:13:12.600
which are relatively
poor countries,

00:13:12.600 --> 00:13:16.110
are not getting rich
very fast, actually

00:13:16.110 --> 00:13:17.500
you don't see much of a change.

00:13:17.500 --> 00:13:18.750
They're about where they were.

00:13:21.880 --> 00:13:24.590
Under five is about what they
are in the adult population.

00:13:24.590 --> 00:13:26.590
So that suggests that
they aren't getting worse.

00:13:26.590 --> 00:13:28.340
It's the richer countries
that are getting

00:13:28.340 --> 00:13:29.591
richer are getting worse.

00:13:29.591 --> 00:13:30.091
Yep?

00:13:30.091 --> 00:13:31.966
AUDIENCE: But wouldn't
you expect this number

00:13:31.966 --> 00:13:35.092
to be higher than the
overall population

00:13:35.092 --> 00:13:38.975
because women are more likely
to live longer and more

00:13:38.975 --> 00:13:40.343
[INAUDIBLE].

00:13:40.343 --> 00:13:41.760
PROFESSOR: That's
absolutely true.

00:13:41.760 --> 00:13:45.700
So 121 go to 120.

00:13:45.700 --> 00:13:47.930
120 is going to go to 115.

00:13:47.930 --> 00:13:49.300
That's about the difference.

00:13:49.300 --> 00:13:52.030
So 120 is going to do 115 of 14.

00:13:52.030 --> 00:13:57.840
But 114 is a lot worse than 106.

00:13:57.840 --> 00:14:00.332
All of the trends are bad.

00:14:00.332 --> 00:14:02.940
Just to understand,
this is a big problem.

00:14:02.940 --> 00:14:06.080
And it's not one that's
getting solved on its own

00:14:06.080 --> 00:14:09.490
as far as I can tell.

00:14:09.490 --> 00:14:11.530
So here's another
way to look at it.

00:14:11.530 --> 00:14:13.455
So this is India and China.

00:14:16.640 --> 00:14:18.600
Give yourself a few
minutes to look at this.

00:14:18.600 --> 00:14:21.910
So the darkest areas
are the worst areas.

00:14:36.080 --> 00:14:39.590
Does anybody know which are
the richer areas of China?

00:14:47.988 --> 00:14:49.964
AUDIENCE: The black areas?

00:14:49.964 --> 00:14:53.516
PROFESSOR: Yeah, it's pretty
much exactly the same.

00:14:53.516 --> 00:14:57.500
I think Shanghai is that
gray area right there,

00:14:57.500 --> 00:15:00.040
which is actually doing
surprisingly well.

00:15:03.770 --> 00:15:09.730
The really poor areas of China
are those very light areas

00:15:09.730 --> 00:15:11.550
in the west.

00:15:11.550 --> 00:15:14.815
That's the Gobi Desert,
the Taklamakan Desert,

00:15:14.815 --> 00:15:16.480
the Chinkiang.

00:15:16.480 --> 00:15:23.140
All the areas with large Muslim
populations are in the east.

00:15:23.140 --> 00:15:25.020
These are the poorest
parts of China,

00:15:25.020 --> 00:15:29.020
and they're the ones which
are doing by far the best.

00:15:29.020 --> 00:15:32.510
The areas that are being really
badly are the most prosperous.

00:15:32.510 --> 00:15:38.180
The dark areas in
the southeast, that's

00:15:38.180 --> 00:15:41.480
like Guangdong, and
Guangzhou, and all these

00:15:41.480 --> 00:15:44.350
really fast-growing,
wealthy provinces

00:15:44.350 --> 00:15:45.820
of China, that's the southeast.

00:15:51.590 --> 00:15:53.470
Yeah?

00:15:53.470 --> 00:15:55.874
AUDIENCE: So I'm not
really sure exactly how

00:15:55.874 --> 00:15:59.690
[INAUDIBLE],, but
the fact that there

00:15:59.690 --> 00:16:03.510
is [INAUDIBLE] in the number
of kids that couples can have.

00:16:03.510 --> 00:16:06.420
You know, people might
be deciding on purpose

00:16:06.420 --> 00:16:10.208
to have boys because they
want boys for whatever reason.

00:16:10.208 --> 00:16:13.717
And maybe the people who
make those decision are

00:16:13.717 --> 00:16:16.500
people to have more money,
because to have abortions

00:16:16.500 --> 00:16:17.880
is expensive.

00:16:17.880 --> 00:16:19.880
PROFESSOR: I think that
has a lot to do with it.

00:16:19.880 --> 00:16:20.940
We'll come back to it.

00:16:20.940 --> 00:16:25.670
You're perfectly right
that a lot of this ease

00:16:25.670 --> 00:16:29.656
has something to do with
people's active choices also.

00:16:29.656 --> 00:16:32.150
I'll come back to
that in a little.

00:16:37.880 --> 00:16:41.910
As China gets even richer,
because this is the trend,

00:16:41.910 --> 00:16:44.780
and it's a worrying
trend, let's say.

00:16:44.780 --> 00:16:47.060
Now look at India.

00:16:47.060 --> 00:16:54.383
Again, do you know what those
relatively pale areas are?

00:16:54.383 --> 00:16:55.830
AUDIENCE: South India?

00:16:55.830 --> 00:16:57.280
PROFESSOR: The
South India, which

00:16:57.280 --> 00:17:03.870
is relatively prosperous
and well-educated,

00:17:03.870 --> 00:17:06.466
and East India which is
the poorest and the most

00:17:06.466 --> 00:17:07.424
backward part of India.

00:17:17.700 --> 00:17:21.099
Richest state in India,
large state in India,

00:17:21.099 --> 00:17:29.270
is Maharashtra, which is
kind of the striped state.

00:17:29.270 --> 00:17:33.050
Let's see, on the
west coast, if you

00:17:33.050 --> 00:17:36.598
go from the south on the west
coast, the first striped one,

00:17:36.598 --> 00:17:37.390
that's Maharashtra.

00:17:37.390 --> 00:17:38.990
That's the richest.

00:17:38.990 --> 00:17:44.800
The second richest large
state is Punjab And Punjab

00:17:44.800 --> 00:17:49.520
is that piece of black
that's right in the north.

00:17:49.520 --> 00:17:54.330
So that's literally the second
richest, and still recently

00:17:54.330 --> 00:17:57.580
the richest state in India,
is that area in black.

00:17:57.580 --> 00:17:59.420
So again, the same pattern--

00:17:59.420 --> 00:18:03.640
rich states have worse
outcomes, not better outcomes.

00:18:03.640 --> 00:18:10.450
And there's no evidence
of a positive correlation

00:18:10.450 --> 00:18:16.246
between income and
the gender ratio.

00:18:16.246 --> 00:18:19.943
AUDIENCE: Is Punjab the
only state in India that has

00:18:19.943 --> 00:18:23.148
a different religion than
the rest of the others?

00:18:23.148 --> 00:18:24.630
Or would that be a reason?

00:18:24.630 --> 00:18:25.338
PROFESSOR: Maybe.

00:18:30.320 --> 00:18:32.370
What that is is there's
really two states there.

00:18:32.370 --> 00:18:34.060
There is Punjab and Haryana.

00:18:34.060 --> 00:18:36.690
They used to be both
a part of Punjab.

00:18:36.690 --> 00:18:39.120
Now they've been split into
two states for some years.

00:18:39.120 --> 00:18:43.720
But one of those states
is Sikh majority.

00:18:43.720 --> 00:18:45.500
Sikh is a particular
other religion.

00:18:45.500 --> 00:18:47.533
The other is Hindu majority.

00:18:47.533 --> 00:18:48.450
There's no difference.

00:18:48.450 --> 00:18:50.750
Actually, Haryana's
slightly worse that Punjab.

00:18:50.750 --> 00:18:54.500
Delhi City is one of the worst
places, actually, in India.

00:18:54.500 --> 00:18:56.950
So that's one of the
absolutely riches places.

00:18:56.950 --> 00:19:01.250
So basically no evidence
of a positive correlation

00:19:01.250 --> 00:19:05.440
between income and
the gender ratio--

00:19:05.440 --> 00:19:09.660
gender ratio, if anything,
gets worse as you get richer,

00:19:09.660 --> 00:19:12.220
for reasons that may
very be close to what

00:19:12.220 --> 00:19:13.270
you're suggesting.

00:19:13.270 --> 00:19:17.080
But it's clear that you get
this rather dismal pattern.

00:19:19.800 --> 00:19:24.900
Here's something else, another
fact about sort of China.

00:19:24.900 --> 00:19:27.580
And it's sort of a very
long term view of China.

00:19:32.310 --> 00:19:35.680
This starts in 1920.

00:19:35.680 --> 00:19:47.120
And you can see that basically
from the 1920s the excess sex

00:19:47.120 --> 00:19:54.380
ratio, which is in the
five-year birth cohort.

00:19:54.380 --> 00:19:58.990
So people who were born
in that year, what's

00:19:58.990 --> 00:20:01.280
the fraction of
boys versus girls?

00:20:01.280 --> 00:20:09.320
So it used to be about
7% more boys than girls.

00:20:09.320 --> 00:20:13.590
Then it went up to about
16% during the years

00:20:13.590 --> 00:20:16.730
when there's a lot of
political instability.

00:20:16.730 --> 00:20:19.830
So if you think of those
years as Japanese invasion,

00:20:19.830 --> 00:20:22.990
before that and after that, in
a sense, China is in civil war.

00:20:26.150 --> 00:20:28.910
What are called the warlords
are fighting each other

00:20:28.910 --> 00:20:29.780
through China.

00:20:29.780 --> 00:20:33.053
So during wars, you see
this ratio going way up.

00:20:33.053 --> 00:20:35.470
Then it comes in down, down,
down, down, down, down, down,

00:20:35.470 --> 00:20:41.820
down, till about
1976 or something.

00:20:41.820 --> 00:20:47.310
Now right around 1976
something else happens.

00:20:47.310 --> 00:20:50.060
China introduces what's
called the one child policy.

00:20:50.060 --> 00:20:55.180
The one child policy is one
where at least in the richer

00:20:55.180 --> 00:20:58.850
areas, where it was the
only place it was enforced,

00:20:58.850 --> 00:21:02.146
families were allowed to
have only one child at most.

00:21:05.410 --> 00:21:10.350
Then that policy gets
implemented in the mid '70s.

00:21:10.350 --> 00:21:11.950
And you see what's
happened since.

00:21:11.950 --> 00:21:15.220
What's striking is it doesn't
just go up and stay up.

00:21:15.220 --> 00:21:18.180
It keeps going up.

00:21:18.180 --> 00:21:20.810
So there's something else
going on on top of the fact

00:21:20.810 --> 00:21:23.030
that the one child
policy gets instituted.

00:21:23.030 --> 00:21:25.760
In fact, over time, the one
child policy has been relaxed.

00:21:29.670 --> 00:21:33.270
And this reflects some of
the tests of the population

00:21:33.270 --> 00:21:33.920
clearly.

00:21:33.920 --> 00:21:37.980
Families which had one
daughter in some areas

00:21:37.980 --> 00:21:42.750
were allowed to have a son
or have a second child more

00:21:42.750 --> 00:21:43.250
recently.

00:21:43.250 --> 00:21:45.500
So there's some attempt
to relax the policy

00:21:45.500 --> 00:21:48.330
in exactly the direction
that suggests that there's

00:21:48.330 --> 00:21:51.150
a preference for boys.

00:21:51.150 --> 00:21:55.360
But with all that,
the trend has been up.

00:21:55.360 --> 00:21:56.240
Yep?

00:21:56.240 --> 00:21:58.165
AUDIENCE: Do you know what's
happened to those policies?

00:21:58.165 --> 00:22:00.082
Were they implemented,
[INAUDIBLE] relaxation?

00:22:03.985 --> 00:22:05.860
PROFESSOR: A bunch to
the relaxations happen.

00:22:05.860 --> 00:22:08.373
You see they're
flattening in about 1982.

00:22:08.373 --> 00:22:09.540
That's the first relaxation.

00:22:12.500 --> 00:22:17.680
So you do see a flattening,
but the trend is bad.

00:22:17.680 --> 00:22:18.680
Yeah?

00:22:18.680 --> 00:22:20.430
AUDIENCE: Even if it's
being relaxed, just

00:22:20.430 --> 00:22:23.236
being relaxed, if the
first kid that you have

00:22:23.236 --> 00:22:26.118
is a girl, and
then in that case,

00:22:26.118 --> 00:22:27.493
you might then
try to have a boy?

00:22:27.493 --> 00:22:29.385
But if they had
the boy right away,

00:22:29.385 --> 00:22:31.500
maybe they'd just
stop having kids.

00:22:31.500 --> 00:22:33.290
PROFESSOR: Absolutely.

00:22:33.290 --> 00:22:36.060
Actually, let's take it.

00:22:36.060 --> 00:22:42.220
So could this be generated
by a stopping rule?

00:22:42.220 --> 00:22:45.730
So imagine that families have
a stopping rule which says,

00:22:45.730 --> 00:22:54.810
we will stop when we
have the first boy.

00:22:54.810 --> 00:22:56.410
This is their stopping rule.

00:22:56.410 --> 00:22:57.930
This is a plausible
stopping rule.

00:22:57.930 --> 00:23:05.450
My mother has one brother
and three sisters.

00:23:05.450 --> 00:23:07.390
The brother's the youngest.

00:23:07.390 --> 00:23:10.640
So that's not an
implausible stopping rule.

00:23:10.640 --> 00:23:15.250
My mother's only brother has
three daughters and once son.

00:23:15.250 --> 00:23:17.280
The son is the youngest.

00:23:17.280 --> 00:23:19.660
It's not an implausible
stopping rule.

00:23:19.660 --> 00:23:26.240
However, imagine we
had this stopping rule.

00:23:26.240 --> 00:23:32.790
But in any but, the probability
of boy and girl would equal.

00:23:32.790 --> 00:23:37.180
What would be the
population sex ratio?

00:23:37.180 --> 00:23:38.644
AUDIENCE: Still the same.

00:23:38.644 --> 00:23:40.061
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
boys, right?

00:23:40.061 --> 00:23:41.572
No, you'd have more women.

00:23:41.572 --> 00:23:43.524
AUDIENCE: No, it's half.

00:23:43.524 --> 00:23:44.988
PROFESSOR: Which one?

00:23:44.988 --> 00:23:48.892
AUDIENCE: If you keep having
children when you have women,

00:23:48.892 --> 00:23:51.702
then you stop as soon
as you have one boy,

00:23:51.702 --> 00:23:55.174
you'd have more women, or
you would expect more women.

00:23:55.174 --> 00:23:56.241
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:23:56.241 --> 00:23:57.158
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:24:00.134 --> 00:24:01.622
You stop immediately.

00:24:01.622 --> 00:24:04.102
You stop immediately
if you have one son.

00:24:04.102 --> 00:24:07.078
[INAUDIBLE]

00:24:07.078 --> 00:24:08.896
There might be a
family where there

00:24:08.896 --> 00:24:10.054
are more girls [INAUDIBLE].

00:24:13.285 --> 00:24:17.650
But most families
will stop there.

00:24:17.650 --> 00:24:20.090
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:24:20.090 --> 00:24:23.818
PROFESSOR: So do you think
they'll be more [INAUDIBLE]??

00:24:23.818 --> 00:24:26.671
AUDIENCE: I think it
pretty much evens out.

00:24:26.671 --> 00:24:29.046
AUDIENCE: I think there will
be more boys, because if you

00:24:29.046 --> 00:24:30.292
have a boy, you just stop.

00:24:30.292 --> 00:24:31.786
So then that family
just has a boy.

00:24:31.786 --> 00:24:34.276
And if you have a girl
and then you try again,

00:24:34.276 --> 00:24:36.301
if you have a boy,
then right now

00:24:36.301 --> 00:24:38.426
you have one family with
one boy and another family

00:24:38.426 --> 00:24:39.343
with a girl and a boy.

00:24:39.343 --> 00:24:41.248
But there's still
more boys than girls.

00:24:41.248 --> 00:24:43.490
So whenever you get
to a boy, you stop.

00:24:43.490 --> 00:24:45.378
PROFESSOR: You want
to come back to that?

00:24:45.378 --> 00:24:48.035
AUDIENCE: Yeah, and so if we
just do an expected values,

00:24:48.035 --> 00:24:49.826
and you get half.

00:24:49.826 --> 00:24:52.316
Half the cases
you'll have one boy.

00:24:52.316 --> 00:24:54.137
What are the cases
of girl and boy?

00:24:54.137 --> 00:24:55.470
PROFESSOR: You're exactly right.

00:24:55.470 --> 00:24:57.640
The answer is it
doesn't change anything.

00:24:57.640 --> 00:24:58.900
And it's very simple to see.

00:24:58.900 --> 00:25:01.570
The reason why it doesn't
change anything is very simple.

00:25:01.570 --> 00:25:06.930
Which is that every but has
a 50% chance of having a boy.

00:25:06.930 --> 00:25:08.340
So just take the number buts.

00:25:08.340 --> 00:25:09.620
Forget about families.

00:25:09.620 --> 00:25:11.460
There's a certain
number of buts.

00:25:11.460 --> 00:25:13.890
Let's say 50 million buts.

00:25:13.890 --> 00:25:16.680
25 million of those
buts will be boys.

00:25:16.680 --> 00:25:18.580
And 25 million of those
buts with be girls.

00:25:18.580 --> 00:25:20.800
It doesn't matter which
family it happens.

00:25:20.800 --> 00:25:23.810
Every but is a separate,
independent event

00:25:23.810 --> 00:25:26.080
and has a 50% chance
of being a boy.

00:25:26.080 --> 00:25:29.440
So paradoxical as it
sounds, stopping girls

00:25:29.440 --> 00:25:31.000
don't change the sex ratio.

00:25:31.000 --> 00:25:32.750
They change the distribution.

00:25:32.750 --> 00:25:38.304
Now here's what might be going
on, which is more complication.

00:25:38.304 --> 00:25:38.804
Yeah?

00:25:38.804 --> 00:25:42.790
AUDIENCE: But again, but
the relaxation is now there.

00:25:42.790 --> 00:25:44.242
Like, it's completely relaxed.

00:25:44.242 --> 00:25:45.520
So then you can have it.

00:25:45.520 --> 00:25:48.150
So it won't be true if you can
have as many kids as possible.

00:25:48.150 --> 00:25:49.942
This is going to make
a difference, though,

00:25:49.942 --> 00:25:51.910
if you have to stall--

00:25:51.910 --> 00:25:57.326
PROFESSOR: As long as you don't
determine the sex of the child

00:25:57.326 --> 00:26:01.260
directly, it doesn't matter.

00:26:01.260 --> 00:26:03.197
Every but is an
independent event

00:26:03.197 --> 00:26:05.780
with a 50% chance
of being a boy.

00:26:05.780 --> 00:26:08.260
So take the number of
bus, multiply it by 50%,

00:26:08.260 --> 00:26:09.587
you're going to get the number.

00:26:09.587 --> 00:26:10.670
So it makes no difference.

00:26:10.670 --> 00:26:15.000
Now let's think of one more
step in the [INAUDIBLE]..

00:26:15.000 --> 00:26:20.420
Which families would a
lot of girls grow up in?

00:26:20.420 --> 00:26:22.440
Would they have more
children or less children?

00:26:22.440 --> 00:26:25.160
Would girls grow up on
average in a family with more

00:26:25.160 --> 00:26:26.356
children or less children?

00:26:26.356 --> 00:26:27.356
AUDIENCE: More children.

00:26:27.356 --> 00:26:28.490
PROFESSOR: More children.

00:26:28.490 --> 00:26:30.698
So if you think that families
that have more children

00:26:30.698 --> 00:26:36.860
have less resources, then
you have a stopping rule,

00:26:36.860 --> 00:26:39.620
then girls would be
growing up in families

00:26:39.620 --> 00:26:43.550
with more children, and
therefore less resources.

00:26:43.550 --> 00:26:48.330
And therefore, a
stopping rule can

00:26:48.330 --> 00:26:54.800
have an effect on
child outcomes,

00:26:54.800 --> 00:26:58.750
because it leads to girls
all living in large families

00:26:58.750 --> 00:27:00.970
and boys all living
in small families.

00:27:00.970 --> 00:27:05.480
So boys get all the attention,
the parent, the resources.

00:27:05.480 --> 00:27:12.170
The seven girls and one
boy in the girl families,

00:27:12.170 --> 00:27:15.340
and therefore the girls end up
with getting less resources.

00:27:15.340 --> 00:27:17.140
So that's one
possible reason why

00:27:17.140 --> 00:27:18.930
you might get this
outcome, because it

00:27:18.930 --> 00:27:21.210
has something to do
with the stopping rule,

00:27:21.210 --> 00:27:22.143
but not directly.

00:27:22.143 --> 00:27:23.810
It has to do with the
stopping rule then

00:27:23.810 --> 00:27:27.180
generates an allocation
of girls across families.

00:27:27.180 --> 00:27:28.956
Yeah?

00:27:28.956 --> 00:27:30.206
AUDIENCE: So that's also true.

00:27:30.206 --> 00:27:34.452
But If you think of about
even with the relaxation,

00:27:34.452 --> 00:27:38.673
imagine a family in
a rich area where

00:27:38.673 --> 00:27:39.840
everything's more expensive.

00:27:39.840 --> 00:27:42.760
Let's say even if it was
a little more relaxed,

00:27:42.760 --> 00:27:44.390
they still limit
themselves to say,

00:27:44.390 --> 00:27:46.750
OK, we're going to have
a maximum of two kids.

00:27:46.750 --> 00:27:50.120
So let's say, yeah,
maybe the first kid

00:27:50.120 --> 00:27:53.810
that they have is a
girl, but then they say,

00:27:53.810 --> 00:27:56.745
the probability of having
a girl for the next one,

00:27:56.745 --> 00:27:59.860
maybe it's not really
50%, because they say,

00:27:59.860 --> 00:28:03.130
well, we're only going to
have this kid if it's a boy.

00:28:03.130 --> 00:28:06.145
PROFESSOR: Yeah, so what you're
saying is completely right.

00:28:06.145 --> 00:28:09.090
The only way you
get away from 50-50

00:28:09.090 --> 00:28:12.300
is by deliberately or
at least doing something

00:28:12.300 --> 00:28:15.100
to the girl that
makes her not live.

00:28:15.100 --> 00:28:18.330
That's the only
way you get there.

00:28:18.330 --> 00:28:22.200
If the girls who are
born live, then you

00:28:22.200 --> 00:28:23.530
can't get away from this.

00:28:23.530 --> 00:28:25.820
It doesn't matter what
your stopping rule is.

00:28:25.820 --> 00:28:28.770
Even if the government
makes complicated policies,

00:28:28.770 --> 00:28:30.390
the policies don't change that.

00:28:30.390 --> 00:28:32.690
Every but is either
a boy or girl.

00:28:36.730 --> 00:28:40.340
So here's the last fact
I wanted you to see,

00:28:40.340 --> 00:28:43.173
which is that if you look at
these sex ratios in India,

00:28:43.173 --> 00:28:44.090
they're getting worse.

00:28:44.090 --> 00:28:46.820
They got worse for a
while, now they have

00:28:46.820 --> 00:28:49.460
stabilized at a low number.

00:28:49.460 --> 00:28:54.710
So in 1900, there were 972
girls for boys, men for women,

00:28:54.710 --> 00:28:56.260
women for men.

00:28:56.260 --> 00:28:59.960
Now there are 933.

00:28:59.960 --> 00:29:05.680
In India, you don't see a
trend after 1971 or 1981.

00:29:05.680 --> 00:29:13.430
But the historical
trend is very negative.

00:29:16.210 --> 00:29:20.480
So that brings us to
sort of the question.

00:29:20.480 --> 00:29:22.510
Before we get to
policy, in a sense

00:29:22.510 --> 00:29:24.320
we need to know what's going on.

00:29:24.320 --> 00:29:26.740
What's going on-- there
are two possible answers.

00:29:26.740 --> 00:29:31.010
One is kind of tradition,
and the other is economics.

00:29:31.010 --> 00:29:35.520
So one is there's
a set of traditions

00:29:35.520 --> 00:29:38.570
which make bad things happen.

00:29:38.570 --> 00:29:40.760
But people are not
willing them to happen.

00:29:40.760 --> 00:29:45.720
The other is that people
are actually making choices

00:29:45.720 --> 00:29:46.930
to make these things happen.

00:29:50.250 --> 00:29:52.150
So what we're going to
spend some time doing

00:29:52.150 --> 00:29:54.810
is trying to distinguish
between these two views.

00:29:54.810 --> 00:29:55.533
Is it tradition?

00:29:55.533 --> 00:29:56.200
Is it economics?

00:29:59.760 --> 00:30:03.870
So you could imagine
just traditionally

00:30:03.870 --> 00:30:07.300
there's a theory which says
that girls need less food.

00:30:07.300 --> 00:30:08.190
That's false.

00:30:08.190 --> 00:30:09.600
But you believe it.

00:30:09.600 --> 00:30:14.170
And you don't actually actively
do anything to make them die.

00:30:14.170 --> 00:30:15.700
You have wrong beliefs.

00:30:15.700 --> 00:30:18.590
You think girls need less
food, because adult women

00:30:18.590 --> 00:30:21.000
are smaller than adult men.

00:30:21.000 --> 00:30:23.270
And so you feed your girls less.

00:30:23.270 --> 00:30:27.140
This is not an attempt to kill
them, it's just a tradition.

00:30:27.140 --> 00:30:30.590
But then that could feed
back and have a consequence.

00:30:35.810 --> 00:30:37.440
Problem is how do you test this?

00:30:42.740 --> 00:30:43.732
Yeah?

00:30:43.732 --> 00:30:47.880
AUDIENCE: If you actually
look at fertility rates,

00:30:47.880 --> 00:30:54.080
so if the number of
people that are born

00:30:54.080 --> 00:30:57.880
are essentially all 50-50,
but then within five years

00:30:57.880 --> 00:30:59.308
you would see a
clear difference.

00:30:59.308 --> 00:31:00.975
So maybe at some point
that's happening.

00:31:00.975 --> 00:31:04.655
But even if in turns of birth
rate, if there's a difference,

00:31:04.655 --> 00:31:06.460
then it's something else.

00:31:06.460 --> 00:31:08.870
PROFESSOR: So one
answer to this question

00:31:08.870 --> 00:31:12.820
is if these guys are
not getting born,

00:31:12.820 --> 00:31:15.890
then obviously something
else is going on.

00:31:15.890 --> 00:31:21.910
Now mostly still about
five years, not five,

00:31:21.910 --> 00:31:27.960
15 years ago, you don't see
a big trend in birthrates.

00:31:27.960 --> 00:31:31.180
Now you actually see a
trend in birthrates as well,

00:31:31.180 --> 00:31:36.700
reported butts they're
more and more reported

00:31:36.700 --> 00:31:38.040
boys relative to girls.

00:31:38.040 --> 00:31:39.790
But over 15 years ago,
you don't see that.

00:31:39.790 --> 00:31:43.710
What you did see was more
different mortality rates

00:31:43.710 --> 00:31:46.540
for boys and girls.

00:31:46.540 --> 00:31:49.130
So imagine that you
wanted to test this view.

00:31:49.130 --> 00:31:52.260
Well the problem is
how do you test it?

00:31:52.260 --> 00:31:57.400
You don't actually observe
who eats what in a family.

00:31:57.400 --> 00:32:00.932
So it's hard to imagine you
going and figuring out oh,

00:32:00.932 --> 00:32:02.390
they're not feeding
their daughter.

00:32:02.390 --> 00:32:03.530
They're feeding their son.

00:32:03.530 --> 00:32:07.150
That's just too difficult to
do, because everybody eats out

00:32:07.150 --> 00:32:08.480
of the same cooked food.

00:32:08.480 --> 00:32:10.810
And so it's going to
be very hard to find.

00:32:10.810 --> 00:32:14.000
Somebody had a very good
idea of how to do it.

00:32:14.000 --> 00:32:17.895
We just looked at specialized
adult goods, like cigarettes.

00:32:21.066 --> 00:32:23.390
Your daughter, a
five-year-old daughter,

00:32:23.390 --> 00:32:28.130
doesn't smoke a cigarette, nor
does your five-year-old son.

00:32:28.130 --> 00:32:33.170
So when a son is
born, if there's

00:32:33.170 --> 00:32:35.395
an effect on how
much smoking you do,

00:32:35.395 --> 00:32:44.690
it has nothing to do with the
children, the children smoking.

00:32:44.690 --> 00:32:48.110
So children are not going to
be a direct source of demand.

00:32:48.110 --> 00:32:51.235
So why is that good?

00:32:51.235 --> 00:32:52.610
Because then you
can look at what

00:32:52.610 --> 00:33:00.550
happens to smoking when the
family gets a boy or a girl.

00:33:00.550 --> 00:33:03.905
And if girls are fed less,
what would you expect?

00:33:08.016 --> 00:33:09.462
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:33:09.462 --> 00:33:12.245
They would smoke more cigarettes
because they have a larger

00:33:12.245 --> 00:33:14.120
share of their budget
to spend on cigarettes,

00:33:14.120 --> 00:33:16.210
because they'll give
less to the girl.

00:33:16.210 --> 00:33:20.040
PROFESSOR: Right, smoking
goes down by less.

00:33:20.040 --> 00:33:22.500
So the basic observation
you should see

00:33:22.500 --> 00:33:25.930
is when a child is born,
adult income goes down,

00:33:25.930 --> 00:33:29.300
because adults have less
money to spend on themselves,

00:33:29.300 --> 00:33:31.120
because they will
feed the child.

00:33:31.120 --> 00:33:35.360
But if they feed the
girl less than the boy,

00:33:35.360 --> 00:33:42.720
then you'd see that
when a boy is born,

00:33:42.720 --> 00:33:44.610
they'll really cut
back on cigarettes.

00:33:44.610 --> 00:33:47.950
But when a girl is born,
they'll cut back less.

00:33:47.950 --> 00:33:51.970
Because they are
feeding the girl less.

00:33:51.970 --> 00:33:54.890
We have a pretty
precise measurement

00:33:54.890 --> 00:33:57.730
of demand for adult goods.

00:33:57.730 --> 00:33:58.800
So we can use that.

00:33:58.800 --> 00:34:00.540
We don't know who smokes.

00:34:00.540 --> 00:34:03.080
But we know that the kids
are not doing the smoking.

00:34:03.080 --> 00:34:05.340
So therefore, we
can by observing

00:34:05.340 --> 00:34:08.420
what happens to a family
when the child is born.

00:34:08.420 --> 00:34:10.440
We can back out
whether or not there

00:34:10.440 --> 00:34:17.219
is a difference in the way
you feed boys or girls.

00:34:17.219 --> 00:34:20.920
Is that clear why that's
better than trying to measure?

00:34:20.920 --> 00:34:23.199
It's very hard to
measure who eats what.

00:34:23.199 --> 00:34:28.261
But clearly the kids
don't smoke cigarettes.

00:34:28.261 --> 00:34:29.469
And they don't drink alcohol.

00:34:29.469 --> 00:34:31.449
So if you look at
alcohol, and cigarettes,

00:34:31.449 --> 00:34:34.800
and a few other things that
are essentially adult goods,

00:34:34.800 --> 00:34:37.730
we should be able to
back out what's happening

00:34:37.730 --> 00:34:38.780
when the child is born.

00:34:52.219 --> 00:34:53.215
Somebody did that.

00:35:01.890 --> 00:35:03.500
So take beverages.

00:35:03.500 --> 00:35:06.910
Beverages doesn't include milk.

00:35:06.910 --> 00:35:12.330
So it's soda, and tea,
and all of those things.

00:35:16.500 --> 00:35:18.590
This table is horrible,
but this paper

00:35:18.590 --> 00:35:26.260
was published in a obscure
journal many years ago.

00:35:26.260 --> 00:35:27.660
This is just a scan of that.

00:35:27.660 --> 00:35:29.120
There's nothing
else I could find.

00:35:29.120 --> 00:35:30.828
There's no electronic
version, et cetera.

00:35:33.530 --> 00:35:38.100
What you're looking for is the
effect of a boy versus a girl,

00:35:38.100 --> 00:35:42.365
so number of males
between zero and four,

00:35:42.365 --> 00:35:44.490
versus the number of females
between zero and four,

00:35:44.490 --> 00:35:49.350
and the effect of that on
the consumption of beverages.

00:35:49.350 --> 00:35:51.300
And the answer is it
has no effect, that

00:35:51.300 --> 00:35:53.250
what's in the bracket
is the tea statistic.

00:35:53.250 --> 00:35:54.440
The tea statistic is zero.

00:35:59.600 --> 00:36:01.092
Both the numbers are zero.

00:36:01.092 --> 00:36:02.550
And that's basically
what you find.

00:36:08.570 --> 00:36:10.790
You see what you'd
expect, which is

00:36:10.790 --> 00:36:17.090
that, for example, when a child
is born, you're just poorer.

00:36:17.090 --> 00:36:21.420
So you buy less
rice, less wheat.

00:36:21.420 --> 00:36:24.750
But there's no difference
between girls and boys.

00:36:24.750 --> 00:36:27.907
Boys and girls have the same
effect, if anything, on both.

00:36:32.730 --> 00:36:38.790
Basically there's no evidence
that when boy child is born,

00:36:38.790 --> 00:36:42.220
families cut back more than
when a girl child is born.

00:36:42.220 --> 00:36:43.970
We don't find any evidence.

00:36:43.970 --> 00:36:46.280
And this will be looked
in many countries.

00:36:46.280 --> 00:36:50.410
So another tradition
story is the following.

00:36:50.410 --> 00:36:55.500
Imagine that tradition is
when the child gets sick,

00:36:55.500 --> 00:36:57.650
do I take it to the hospital?

00:36:57.650 --> 00:36:59.750
And with the boy, the
tradition is I always

00:36:59.750 --> 00:37:00.830
take it to the hospital.

00:37:00.830 --> 00:37:04.870
With a girl, I just
give her some water

00:37:04.870 --> 00:37:10.505
and generally sing
songs to her, but I

00:37:10.505 --> 00:37:15.900
don't take her to the
hospital, something like that.

00:37:15.900 --> 00:37:18.890
Do we see any evidence of this?

00:37:18.890 --> 00:37:24.360
So this is hard to study,
because we don't actually

00:37:24.360 --> 00:37:29.000
observe that many episodes
of life-threatening diseases.

00:37:29.000 --> 00:37:33.677
But there's a nice fact
that Elaina Rose identified,

00:37:33.677 --> 00:37:34.635
which is the following.

00:37:40.330 --> 00:37:44.560
Over time, different
parts of India

00:37:44.560 --> 00:37:47.370
have droughts at
different points of time.

00:37:47.370 --> 00:37:54.120
So some areas have
droughts in 2006.

00:37:54.120 --> 00:37:55.730
Some have droughts in 2004.

00:37:55.730 --> 00:37:58.100
Some have droughts in 1996.

00:37:58.100 --> 00:38:01.005
So a drought is a time
of economic stress.

00:38:07.280 --> 00:38:10.207
What's a drought?

00:38:10.207 --> 00:38:11.290
Why is it economic stress?

00:38:14.629 --> 00:38:17.491
AUDIENCE: Lower end
qualities to low crop yield.

00:38:17.491 --> 00:38:20.200
PROFESSOR: Yeah, basically
you can't grow anything.

00:38:20.200 --> 00:38:21.340
It's too dry.

00:38:21.340 --> 00:38:26.100
So when the rains
fail, crops fail.

00:38:26.100 --> 00:38:27.480
Families have no money.

00:38:31.890 --> 00:38:36.450
Well, how are they dealing
with this particular emergency?

00:38:36.450 --> 00:38:38.830
And there she
finds some evidence

00:38:38.830 --> 00:38:42.530
that relative to non-drought
years, in drought years,

00:38:42.530 --> 00:38:45.640
girls die more.

00:38:45.640 --> 00:38:47.640
So that suggests
that emergencies

00:38:47.640 --> 00:38:49.440
might be a part of the story.

00:38:49.440 --> 00:38:53.140
In emergencies, girls
are not treated as well.

00:38:53.140 --> 00:38:57.570
So I gave one example, which is
not taking her to the hospital.

00:38:57.570 --> 00:38:59.910
The other one might be,
when I don't have money,

00:38:59.910 --> 00:39:03.350
I don't buy food for her.

00:39:03.350 --> 00:39:07.160
So some tradition of that
kind might explain it.

00:39:07.160 --> 00:39:09.850
Problem is that droughts
are not frequent enough.

00:39:09.850 --> 00:39:11.600
And remember the trends.

00:39:11.600 --> 00:39:13.020
The trends are all bad.

00:39:13.020 --> 00:39:15.910
They're all getting worse.

00:39:15.910 --> 00:39:17.610
India and China
are getting richer.

00:39:17.610 --> 00:39:20.090
The dependence on
agriculture in general,

00:39:20.090 --> 00:39:24.560
these kinds of extreme
events, is just going down.

00:39:24.560 --> 00:39:27.160
Droughts don't matter
as much in India now.

00:39:29.890 --> 00:39:33.160
30 years ago, agriculture
was 50% of GDP.

00:39:33.160 --> 00:39:35.010
Now it's 25% of GDP.

00:39:35.010 --> 00:39:38.060
So basically, droughts
matter less to people.

00:39:38.060 --> 00:39:40.420
And since droughts
matter less to people,

00:39:40.420 --> 00:39:42.760
you would imagine the
trend being favorable

00:39:42.760 --> 00:39:43.940
rather than against.

00:39:43.940 --> 00:39:45.320
Think of China.

00:39:45.320 --> 00:39:48.850
Most people are now in industry.

00:39:48.850 --> 00:39:50.238
They're also richer.

00:39:50.238 --> 00:39:52.780
And as you get richer, and you
would imagine that emergencies

00:39:52.780 --> 00:39:53.900
are less serious.

00:39:53.900 --> 00:39:55.850
Because you always
have some money.

00:39:55.850 --> 00:39:58.520
You're no longer at the
margin of starvation.

00:39:58.520 --> 00:40:00.530
So the problem
with this story is

00:40:00.530 --> 00:40:03.360
that it's just inconsistent with
the fact the things are getting

00:40:03.360 --> 00:40:05.090
worse over time.

00:40:05.090 --> 00:40:06.815
You would think that
under this story

00:40:06.815 --> 00:40:09.190
things would be getting better,
because it's an emergency

00:40:09.190 --> 00:40:09.940
story.

00:40:09.940 --> 00:40:13.620
And as countries get richer
and less exposed to risk,

00:40:13.620 --> 00:40:15.620
you would see less and
less of this, rather than

00:40:15.620 --> 00:40:17.300
more and more.

00:40:17.300 --> 00:40:19.465
So that's why this can't
be the whole explanation.

00:40:19.465 --> 00:40:20.940
It could be a piece of it.

00:40:24.950 --> 00:40:29.560
So going back to our
distinction between kind

00:40:29.560 --> 00:40:37.380
of the tradition-based stories
and the more economic stories,

00:40:37.380 --> 00:40:40.150
here is an economic
fact, which is striking.

00:40:47.660 --> 00:40:50.230
So in other words, what I
mean by an economic story

00:40:50.230 --> 00:40:52.270
is that tradition-based
stories say

00:40:52.270 --> 00:40:56.210
that there's some rule
people use, fixed rule.

00:40:56.210 --> 00:40:57.670
They have not
decided on this rule.

00:40:57.670 --> 00:40:59.100
This is given to them.

00:40:59.100 --> 00:41:02.170
And that rule somehow,
under some circumstances,

00:41:02.170 --> 00:41:04.590
has bad outcome for girls.

00:41:04.590 --> 00:41:09.387
So when the girl is sick, you
don't take her to the hospital.

00:41:09.387 --> 00:41:09.970
That's a rule.

00:41:12.620 --> 00:41:16.450
We're now asking a question,
when the economic incentives

00:41:16.450 --> 00:41:20.270
to treat girls better changes,
do you treat them better?

00:41:20.270 --> 00:41:23.170
That suggests that you
don't have a fixed rule.

00:41:23.170 --> 00:41:27.220
You're actually behaving
in a rational way,

00:41:27.220 --> 00:41:29.590
quote unquote "rational way."

00:41:29.590 --> 00:41:34.363
You're actually planning to
have bad things happen to girls.

00:41:34.363 --> 00:41:35.780
So do you see what
the distinction

00:41:35.780 --> 00:41:39.810
is between tradition-based
stories and economic stories?

00:41:39.810 --> 00:41:41.650
Economic stories are
ones which essentially

00:41:41.650 --> 00:41:46.750
say that we are making active
choices which have consequences

00:41:46.750 --> 00:41:52.630
for girls, rather than this
is a rule we use and sometimes

00:41:52.630 --> 00:41:56.630
bad things happen, because
we use a particular rule.

00:41:56.630 --> 00:41:58.880
Here is a nice example.

00:41:58.880 --> 00:42:04.920
So in China, after 1979,
agriculture is liberalized,

00:42:04.920 --> 00:42:06.980
meaning people are
allowed to keep the money

00:42:06.980 --> 00:42:09.270
from going their crops.

00:42:09.270 --> 00:42:12.630
This is the start of
China's economic miracle.

00:42:12.630 --> 00:42:15.000
It's called the family
responsibility system,

00:42:15.000 --> 00:42:17.850
and it's sort of when
everything in China starts.

00:42:21.010 --> 00:42:26.130
Tea is one crop where women
have a comparative advantage

00:42:26.130 --> 00:42:27.430
and even absolute advantage.

00:42:27.430 --> 00:42:32.230
Tea is something that has
to be picked very patiently.

00:42:32.230 --> 00:42:36.970
The most valuable leaves in
tea are the smallest ones.

00:42:36.970 --> 00:42:39.160
So here to pick
the small leaves,

00:42:39.160 --> 00:42:41.600
you have to pick them
without bruising them.

00:42:41.600 --> 00:42:44.310
If the leaf is bruised,
the price goes down.

00:42:44.310 --> 00:42:53.360
The best leaf teas, like
Chinese teas, sell for $600

00:42:53.360 --> 00:42:58.180
a kilogram, so something
of the order of magnitude.

00:42:58.180 --> 00:42:59.660
It's pretty expensive.

00:42:59.660 --> 00:43:02.260
So if you actually
have the best teas,

00:43:02.260 --> 00:43:04.570
you bruise a leaf,
that costs money.

00:43:04.570 --> 00:43:06.240
So you better have
somebody who picks

00:43:06.240 --> 00:43:09.275
with very delicate fingers.

00:43:09.275 --> 00:43:10.900
Whether it's true or
not, I don't know,

00:43:10.900 --> 00:43:12.730
but it's believed
in China that women

00:43:12.730 --> 00:43:16.380
are better for picking
expensive tea leaves.

00:43:16.380 --> 00:43:17.410
Maybe they are.

00:43:17.410 --> 00:43:21.745
Maybe this is a story that men
made up to make them do it.

00:43:21.745 --> 00:43:26.130
But whatever the explanation,
this is the belief.

00:43:26.130 --> 00:43:27.980
So given that
belief, what matters

00:43:27.980 --> 00:43:29.530
is what people believe here.

00:43:29.530 --> 00:43:34.160
Because would imagine
that another crop, which

00:43:34.160 --> 00:43:36.780
is the opposite, is fruits.

00:43:36.780 --> 00:43:39.840
Fruits need someone to
climb a tree and pick them.

00:43:39.840 --> 00:43:43.540
And again in China,
the belief is that men

00:43:43.540 --> 00:43:46.770
are better at climbing trees--

00:43:46.770 --> 00:43:49.910
maybe again false.

00:43:49.910 --> 00:43:53.530
So men are better
at picking fruits.

00:43:53.530 --> 00:43:56.460
Women are better at picking tea.

00:43:56.460 --> 00:43:58.390
That's starting fact.

00:43:58.390 --> 00:43:59.730
At that's what people believe.

00:43:59.730 --> 00:44:01.220
Let's start from there.

00:44:01.220 --> 00:44:09.940
Now suppose parents respond
to economic incentives.

00:44:09.940 --> 00:44:12.640
And what would you expect
happens when the price of tea

00:44:12.640 --> 00:44:13.910
goes up?

00:44:13.910 --> 00:44:16.780
What do you think will
happen to their daughter?

00:44:16.780 --> 00:44:17.550
Yeah?

00:44:17.550 --> 00:44:19.020
AUDIENCE: They'll treat
their daughter better.

00:44:19.020 --> 00:44:20.290
PROFESSOR: They'll treat
their daughter better.

00:44:20.290 --> 00:44:22.290
They're going to feed
her more, or they're

00:44:22.290 --> 00:44:27.480
going to take her to the
hospital more willingly,

00:44:27.480 --> 00:44:28.640
et cetera.

00:44:28.640 --> 00:44:29.790
So you'd expect that.

00:44:29.790 --> 00:44:34.440
And likewise, when the
price of fruits goes up,

00:44:34.440 --> 00:44:38.350
you'd expect boys to
be treated better.

00:44:38.350 --> 00:44:43.810
Now in fact, both price
of fruit and trees go up.

00:44:43.810 --> 00:44:45.850
And that's a trend.

00:44:45.850 --> 00:44:48.080
So it's hard to use a
trend to study this.

00:44:48.080 --> 00:44:49.890
Because everything's going up.

00:44:49.890 --> 00:44:51.870
Both tea and fruits
are going up.

00:44:51.870 --> 00:44:54.220
So you can't distinguish
between these two.

00:44:54.220 --> 00:44:56.120
But one thing you
can do is you can

00:44:56.120 --> 00:45:00.860
look at areas which
are suitable for tea,

00:45:00.860 --> 00:45:03.040
areas just suitable
for fruits, and areas

00:45:03.040 --> 00:45:06.670
that were suitable for neither.

00:45:06.670 --> 00:45:10.090
Now you can say when
tea prices go up,

00:45:10.090 --> 00:45:12.630
do we see a bigger
change in the gender

00:45:12.630 --> 00:45:19.600
ratio in the tea-suitable
areas relative to the others?

00:45:19.600 --> 00:45:25.270
Or you can ask, when the
price of fruits go up,

00:45:25.270 --> 00:45:29.020
do we see a bigger change
in the gender ratio

00:45:29.020 --> 00:45:34.450
in the fruit-favored
areas than in the areas

00:45:34.450 --> 00:45:35.860
where neither is favored?

00:45:35.860 --> 00:45:40.160
So we're always comparing areas
where, basically, wheat or rice

00:45:40.160 --> 00:45:44.300
grows with tea areas
and fruit areas.

00:45:44.300 --> 00:45:45.940
Is that clear what we're doing?

00:45:45.940 --> 00:45:49.450
We're asking when the
price of fruits goes up,

00:45:49.450 --> 00:45:52.707
do we see a different trend
in fruit-growing areas,

00:45:52.707 --> 00:45:54.040
relative to wheat-growing areas?

00:45:56.830 --> 00:46:01.530
Is that clear why that's a good
way to look at this question?

00:46:04.230 --> 00:46:05.970
What are we worried about?

00:46:05.970 --> 00:46:09.120
If we just said, well, the
price of tea is going up,

00:46:09.120 --> 00:46:14.170
what's happening to girls?

00:46:14.170 --> 00:46:16.340
Well, the price of
tea is going up,

00:46:16.340 --> 00:46:18.200
but it's going up over time.

00:46:18.200 --> 00:46:21.050
If the fraction of
girls is going down,

00:46:21.050 --> 00:46:23.530
we'll get a negative
association out of that.

00:46:23.530 --> 00:46:25.290
Because there are
two things happening.

00:46:25.290 --> 00:46:28.000
One is that it's just going
down for other reasons.

00:46:28.000 --> 00:46:29.920
The other is prices
of tea is going up

00:46:29.920 --> 00:46:32.057
for whatever economic reasons.

00:46:32.057 --> 00:46:33.390
And I'm going to correlate them.

00:46:33.390 --> 00:46:34.815
I'll find a negative
correlation.

00:46:34.815 --> 00:46:36.190
So that's not what
we want to do.

00:46:36.190 --> 00:46:39.140
We want to look at areas
which are suitable for tea.

00:46:39.140 --> 00:46:40.640
That's where the
price of tea should

00:46:40.640 --> 00:46:43.650
have an effect, should not
have an effect in areas

00:46:43.650 --> 00:46:45.320
which are not suitable for tea.

00:46:45.320 --> 00:46:48.810
So we're going to compare the
effect of an increase in price

00:46:48.810 --> 00:46:52.740
of tea in girls in
tea-suitable areas

00:46:52.740 --> 00:46:57.260
versus non-tea-suitable
areas, not compare trends.

00:46:57.260 --> 00:46:58.855
This is basically
what is called doing

00:46:58.855 --> 00:46:59.980
a difference in difference.

00:46:59.980 --> 00:47:06.100
And we'll take high tea price
periods, low tea price periods,

00:47:06.100 --> 00:47:09.400
and compare tea-suitable areas
and non-tea-suitable areas.

00:47:13.580 --> 00:47:16.430
You look more lost
than I would expect.

00:47:16.430 --> 00:47:19.110
It's a very easy idea.

00:47:19.110 --> 00:47:23.240
Somebody else want to say
back what I'm trying to say?

00:47:23.240 --> 00:47:27.646
Who's going to volunteer to
improve on that explanation?

00:47:27.646 --> 00:47:29.610
I'm going to [INAUDIBLE].

00:47:29.610 --> 00:47:30.592
Somebody [INAUDIBLE]?

00:47:35.993 --> 00:47:37.073
OK, go ahead.

00:47:37.073 --> 00:47:38.448
AUDIENCE: Basically
you're trying

00:47:38.448 --> 00:47:41.394
to see that if
this does actually

00:47:41.394 --> 00:47:44.340
have an effect on how
parents treat children.

00:47:44.340 --> 00:47:48.035
You'll see girls treated
better in places where

00:47:48.035 --> 00:47:52.740
tea is the cash crop or
something and the price

00:47:52.740 --> 00:47:55.680
increases, versus areas where
tea is not the cash crop,

00:47:55.680 --> 00:47:57.150
but the price increases.

00:47:57.150 --> 00:47:59.600
There shouldn't be a change
in how the girls are treated.

00:47:59.600 --> 00:48:00.392
PROFESSOR: Correct.

00:48:00.392 --> 00:48:04.460
So we're going to compare years
when tea prices went up with

00:48:04.460 --> 00:48:09.800
years when tea prices didn't
go up and compare them in areas

00:48:09.800 --> 00:48:13.640
where tea is a plausible crop
and areas where tea cannot be

00:48:13.640 --> 00:48:15.770
grown.

00:48:15.770 --> 00:48:19.095
So the increase in tea prices
should have no effect where tea

00:48:19.095 --> 00:48:21.100
cannot be grown.

00:48:21.100 --> 00:48:24.620
It will have a spurious effect,
maybe, but no really effect.

00:48:24.620 --> 00:48:26.620
It should have an effect
where tea can be grown.

00:48:26.620 --> 00:48:32.010
So we're going to compare
tea possible areas with other

00:48:32.010 --> 00:48:35.542
areas, when tea prices go
up versus when it doesn't.

00:48:35.542 --> 00:48:36.150
Yeah?

00:48:36.150 --> 00:48:37.692
AUDIENCE: How about
we think about it

00:48:37.692 --> 00:48:39.934
just like you have you
control population,

00:48:39.934 --> 00:48:43.245
where the tea price going
up shouldn't have an effect.

00:48:43.245 --> 00:48:44.720
And then you have
your non-control,

00:48:44.720 --> 00:48:47.352
and you just take you data and
subtract your control from it

00:48:47.352 --> 00:48:48.310
to see you [INAUDIBLE].

00:48:48.310 --> 00:48:49.643
PROFESSOR: That's exactly right.

00:48:49.643 --> 00:48:51.950
So that's another way
to say the same thing.

00:48:51.950 --> 00:48:54.840
Anybody else want to
add anything to that?

00:48:54.840 --> 00:48:58.040
So let me show you some trends.

00:48:58.040 --> 00:49:01.950
So that's the basic fact.

00:49:01.950 --> 00:49:06.430
So that's tea prices.

00:49:06.430 --> 00:49:10.400
Suddenly in '79, you see stock
tea prices just skyrocketed.

00:49:10.400 --> 00:49:12.820
That's the first fact.

00:49:12.820 --> 00:49:14.420
Tea prices are going up.

00:49:14.420 --> 00:49:16.360
Tea production is going up.

00:49:16.360 --> 00:49:20.910
Second thing is that if you
look at tea versus other crops,

00:49:20.910 --> 00:49:23.510
tea prices are going up
faster than other crops.

00:49:23.510 --> 00:49:25.400
So other crops are like grain.

00:49:25.400 --> 00:49:29.070
Grain is going up, but
tea is going up faster.

00:49:29.070 --> 00:49:31.830
Oil is going up, but
tea is going up faster.

00:49:31.830 --> 00:49:37.750
So tea is the crop that is going
up fastest among these crops.

00:49:37.750 --> 00:49:39.450
Look at this one.

00:49:39.450 --> 00:49:42.680
Here we're comparing
fruits with tea.

00:49:42.680 --> 00:49:45.000
And fruits are going
up much faster.

00:49:45.000 --> 00:49:49.950
Fruits with other crops,
like grain, oil, and cotton,

00:49:49.950 --> 00:49:52.310
and again, fruits are
going up much faster.

00:49:52.310 --> 00:49:57.360
So basically, what matters
is not the absolute price.

00:49:57.360 --> 00:50:02.000
The relative price
matters, because it's what

00:50:02.000 --> 00:50:04.280
makes you want to do more tea.

00:50:04.280 --> 00:50:07.330
So tea areas are benefiting,
relative to those other areas.

00:50:13.190 --> 00:50:16.560
So this is a complicated
graph, so let's do this slowly.

00:50:16.560 --> 00:50:19.010
Try to understand
what's in this graph.

00:50:19.010 --> 00:50:27.690
So what's in this graph is
areas which grow a lot of tea.

00:50:35.240 --> 00:50:38.055
This is the coefficient
in the regression.

00:50:41.610 --> 00:50:48.820
So this is the effect of
having more area devoted

00:50:48.820 --> 00:50:50.970
to tea in a particular year.

00:50:50.970 --> 00:50:59.020
So what this is saying
is that before 1979, you

00:50:59.020 --> 00:51:01.650
see tea prices are low.

00:51:01.650 --> 00:51:07.170
In those years, if you look
at the fraction of males,

00:51:07.170 --> 00:51:09.510
the fraction of
males bounces around.

00:51:09.510 --> 00:51:16.700
But the fact that tea is sown
has essentially very close

00:51:16.700 --> 00:51:19.110
to zero effect.

00:51:19.110 --> 00:51:23.980
After 1979, the fact that tea is
sown has a much bigger effect.

00:51:23.980 --> 00:51:26.550
So this is the effect
of how much land

00:51:26.550 --> 00:51:31.660
you put into tea in a particular
year, so year by year.

00:51:31.660 --> 00:51:34.550
So in that year,
what's happening,

00:51:34.550 --> 00:51:40.700
in 1983, how many
boys versus girls

00:51:40.700 --> 00:51:48.410
were born in high tea areas
relative to low tea areas?

00:51:48.410 --> 00:51:50.150
That's always the exercise.

00:51:50.150 --> 00:51:56.480
So think of one more percent
of land devoted to tea.

00:51:56.480 --> 00:52:01.380
What is the effect of that
on the fraction of boys

00:52:01.380 --> 00:52:04.940
born in 1983?

00:52:04.940 --> 00:52:07.430
That's what that says.

00:52:07.430 --> 00:52:10.960
So the main point
to take away is

00:52:10.960 --> 00:52:15.110
you see a big drop after 1979.

00:52:15.110 --> 00:52:20.250
So tea areas, the
fraction of boys born

00:52:20.250 --> 00:52:22.850
goes down right when you
think it should go down.

00:52:22.850 --> 00:52:24.637
It is when the price
starts going up.

00:52:27.380 --> 00:52:30.730
So that's what this is saying.

00:52:30.730 --> 00:52:35.390
Before that, the average
is kind of at 0.15.

00:52:35.390 --> 00:52:39.720
After that, the
average is about 0.35.

00:52:39.720 --> 00:52:44.650
So the fraction of boys
goes down after the change.

00:52:44.650 --> 00:52:49.130
This is what happens
in the fruit areas--

00:52:49.130 --> 00:52:51.510
the opposite.

00:52:51.510 --> 00:52:55.330
After '79, fruit prices
go up also a lot.

00:52:55.330 --> 00:52:56.320
And fruit favors boys.

00:52:59.600 --> 00:53:02.170
The ratio used to be lower.

00:53:02.170 --> 00:53:04.983
It goes up afterwards.

00:53:04.983 --> 00:53:05.483
Yeah?

00:53:05.483 --> 00:53:07.150
AUDIENCE: Are these
significant results?

00:53:07.150 --> 00:53:10.910
PROFESSOR: Yes, so the average
is significantly different.

00:53:10.910 --> 00:53:11.850
You're right.

00:53:11.850 --> 00:53:14.230
Year by year, they're
not significant.

00:53:14.230 --> 00:53:16.930
But that's because it's
a small sample of people.

00:53:16.930 --> 00:53:21.100
But if you take the
average of these periods,

00:53:21.100 --> 00:53:22.900
the averages are
significantly different.

00:53:22.900 --> 00:53:24.858
AUDIENCE: I'm just having
trouble understanding

00:53:24.858 --> 00:53:35.330
how so if women are better
at picking tea leaves,

00:53:35.330 --> 00:53:40.260
the economics make sense, but I
can't see families consciously

00:53:40.260 --> 00:53:44.600
choosing to have more girls.

00:53:44.600 --> 00:53:47.720
In 12 years, they'll begin to--

00:53:47.720 --> 00:53:49.290
PROFESSOR: Well, yes and no.

00:53:49.290 --> 00:53:50.450
You're right with that.

00:53:50.450 --> 00:53:52.050
But on the other
hand, it also sends

00:53:52.050 --> 00:53:55.750
a signal which reminds
them, oh well, you know.

00:53:55.750 --> 00:53:58.840
Two things-- one, you might
imagine that the women might

00:53:58.840 --> 00:54:03.780
be more pro-girls, plausibly.

00:54:03.780 --> 00:54:07.240
And if you think that
women income share

00:54:07.240 --> 00:54:08.440
goes up in the family--

00:54:08.440 --> 00:54:09.800
AUDIENCE: They have more stakes.

00:54:09.800 --> 00:54:11.050
PROFESSOR: They have more say.

00:54:11.050 --> 00:54:12.530
So that's one
possible mechanism.

00:54:12.530 --> 00:54:18.940
The second mechanism
is just that maybe when

00:54:18.940 --> 00:54:22.400
girls' incomes go up,
they just look at it

00:54:22.400 --> 00:54:27.710
and say, well, after
all, all the women

00:54:27.710 --> 00:54:28.770
are working these days.

00:54:28.770 --> 00:54:30.090
Why not have another girl?

00:54:30.090 --> 00:54:31.620
They'll be fine.

00:54:31.620 --> 00:54:34.540
They'll find a job as well.

00:54:34.540 --> 00:54:37.880
I can't tell what's
going on at that level.

00:54:37.880 --> 00:54:40.040
But it's clear that this
result is significant.

00:54:40.040 --> 00:54:41.123
It's actually substantial.

00:54:45.550 --> 00:54:48.070
Final set of thoughts--

00:54:52.150 --> 00:54:53.360
suppose this goes on.

00:54:56.085 --> 00:54:57.990
Let me back up.

00:54:57.990 --> 00:54:59.415
What's going on?

00:54:59.415 --> 00:55:01.245
How are they losing
all these girls?

00:55:01.245 --> 00:55:05.570
If it's an economic
mechanism, that

00:55:05.570 --> 00:55:08.360
says that there is
incentive to lose the girls.

00:55:08.360 --> 00:55:11.340
How are they actually doing it?

00:55:11.340 --> 00:55:12.090
What's going on?

00:55:19.754 --> 00:55:24.160
Unfortunately, it's all not
very nice, what's going on.

00:55:24.160 --> 00:55:26.250
So two things are happening.

00:55:26.250 --> 00:55:31.800
One is basically the technology
for pre-birth detection

00:55:31.800 --> 00:55:34.530
of gender of the child
has improved a lot.

00:55:37.160 --> 00:55:41.540
If you do amniocentesis, which
is an expensive procedure,

00:55:41.540 --> 00:55:43.560
you can basically
very high probability

00:55:43.560 --> 00:55:46.070
predict the gender of the child.

00:55:46.070 --> 00:55:49.610
The reason why rich
areas are doing the worst

00:55:49.610 --> 00:55:51.120
is precisely because of that.

00:55:51.120 --> 00:55:53.345
Rich areas are using
this technology.

00:55:56.000 --> 00:55:59.320
It's an expensive technology.

00:55:59.320 --> 00:56:02.230
You spend money to use it.

00:56:02.230 --> 00:56:09.890
You see in like the richer parts
of India, signs up would say,

00:56:09.890 --> 00:56:22.380
would you rather spend basically
$100 now or $10,000 later?

00:56:22.380 --> 00:56:25.660
The idea is that you're to
give dowry for your daughter

00:56:25.660 --> 00:56:26.720
to get her married.

00:56:26.720 --> 00:56:28.000
And that's your cost.

00:56:28.000 --> 00:56:31.740
And so you should really get
ready to spend, whatever,

00:56:31.740 --> 00:56:38.410
$100 now, identify her,
and then have an abortion,

00:56:38.410 --> 00:56:40.380
rather than spend.

00:56:40.380 --> 00:56:41.960
You see these signs up.

00:56:41.960 --> 00:56:44.000
They're all the illegal.

00:56:44.000 --> 00:56:48.490
Amniocentesis about eight years
ago was made illegal in India.

00:56:48.490 --> 00:56:51.010
It's still completely prevalent.

00:56:51.010 --> 00:56:55.420
And since people are willing
to try to have many babies,

00:56:55.420 --> 00:57:00.540
they're even willing to do it by
using a much weaker technology,

00:57:00.540 --> 00:57:01.750
which is a sonography.

00:57:01.750 --> 00:57:06.030
Sonography, which is a
technology that is legal,

00:57:06.030 --> 00:57:09.580
it doesn't identify the gender
of the child all the time,

00:57:09.580 --> 00:57:12.060
but over 25% of
the time, it does.

00:57:12.060 --> 00:57:16.040
And when it does, then you can
immediately abort the child.

00:57:16.040 --> 00:57:18.900
So they're using
technologies to identify.

00:57:18.900 --> 00:57:21.330
This is why this is happening
more in richer areas,

00:57:21.330 --> 00:57:23.390
because technologies cost money.

00:57:23.390 --> 00:57:25.350
And the poorest
areas are ones where

00:57:25.350 --> 00:57:29.580
the technology is least used,
because it's just expensive.

00:57:29.580 --> 00:57:33.550
So you see basically a
very, very clear correlation

00:57:33.550 --> 00:57:36.990
between where the
technology arrives

00:57:36.990 --> 00:57:40.390
and the fraction
of boys being born.

00:57:40.390 --> 00:57:42.210
So that's one thing
that's going on.

00:57:42.210 --> 00:57:48.880
Second thing is going on almost
surely is a lot of post-birth,

00:57:48.880 --> 00:57:51.730
I think, murder is the
only word you could use.

00:57:51.730 --> 00:57:53.780
I think unfortunately
there's a lot of, I think,

00:57:53.780 --> 00:57:56.820
children being disposed of.

00:57:56.820 --> 00:58:01.420
There are villages which report
to have, probably report,

00:58:01.420 --> 00:58:04.370
there's a news report I think
from like the Chinese news

00:58:04.370 --> 00:58:06.290
agency which says
that we haven't

00:58:06.290 --> 00:58:13.900
had a girl born in our village
in the last five years.

00:58:13.900 --> 00:58:17.370
There's a news report of
the village chief proudly

00:58:17.370 --> 00:58:20.290
announcing that there
was no girl born

00:58:20.290 --> 00:58:22.140
in his village in
the last five years.

00:58:22.140 --> 00:58:25.940
So I think there is a lot of
just direct, deliberate action

00:58:25.940 --> 00:58:27.860
going on there.

00:58:27.860 --> 00:58:32.540
Now, there are many things
to think about here.

00:58:32.540 --> 00:58:33.350
How do you stop it?

00:58:33.350 --> 00:58:34.430
The government has tried.

00:58:34.430 --> 00:58:37.210
It tried to have technologies.

00:58:37.210 --> 00:58:39.905
There's a bunch of programs
around now, for example.

00:58:42.460 --> 00:58:45.860
India has a number of states
where they have programs that

00:58:45.860 --> 00:58:51.430
basically promise parents
a certain amount of money

00:58:51.430 --> 00:58:54.380
if your girl graduates
from high school--

00:58:54.380 --> 00:58:55.350
quite a lot of money.

00:58:55.350 --> 00:58:58.260
So that's one way to
[INAUDIBLE] incentives

00:58:58.260 --> 00:58:59.560
based on girls' arrival.

00:59:06.870 --> 00:59:10.000
But it's not clear
how well that works.

00:59:10.000 --> 00:59:14.240
Because in some
ways, it's something

00:59:14.240 --> 00:59:15.940
that's based on 20 years later.

00:59:15.940 --> 00:59:19.210
And you don't necessarily
think that people

00:59:19.210 --> 00:59:21.462
are that responsive to it.

00:59:21.462 --> 00:59:22.920
The other thing
you can think about

00:59:22.920 --> 00:59:30.030
is is there any economic force
that this, by itself, sets off,

00:59:30.030 --> 00:59:33.340
which will then
make re-equilibrium.

00:59:33.340 --> 00:59:36.310
So one issue that
arises is what's

00:59:36.310 --> 00:59:38.150
happening to the
marriage market?

00:59:38.150 --> 00:59:40.150
So what do you think will
happen to the marriage

00:59:40.150 --> 00:59:44.484
market in China?

00:59:44.484 --> 00:59:46.388
AUDIENCE: Well, if
there are fewer women,

00:59:46.388 --> 00:59:49.063
then each one is
essentially worth more.

00:59:49.063 --> 00:59:49.730
PROFESSOR: Yeah.

00:59:53.290 --> 00:59:56.570
Dowry should go down, and
bride price should go up.

01:00:05.600 --> 01:00:07.610
Why this hasn't happened
already in India,

01:00:07.610 --> 01:00:10.960
especially, is that
men tend to marry women

01:00:10.960 --> 01:00:13.914
who are younger than them.

01:00:13.914 --> 01:00:18.000
If the population is
growing, and people

01:00:18.000 --> 01:00:24.730
marry women who are
younger than them, then

01:00:24.730 --> 01:00:28.790
even if there are
fewer girls born,

01:00:28.790 --> 01:00:33.640
the girls are from a different
cohort, from a younger cohort.

01:00:33.640 --> 01:00:36.830
So the population is growing,
so there's more girls around.

01:00:41.220 --> 01:00:42.630
So that's one problem.

01:00:42.630 --> 01:00:44.690
This force might
operate, but only

01:00:44.690 --> 01:00:46.110
if population growth stops.

01:00:46.110 --> 01:00:48.640
So it's going to operate more
in China where the population

01:00:48.640 --> 01:00:51.480
growth has stopped than in India
where the population growth is

01:00:51.480 --> 01:00:52.608
still going on.

01:00:52.608 --> 01:00:54.150
As long as there's
population growth,

01:00:54.150 --> 01:00:56.920
you're going to see this
possibility of just going down

01:00:56.920 --> 01:00:57.975
in the cohort chain.

01:01:01.200 --> 01:01:03.910
So you might imagine that the
price of girls will go up,

01:01:03.910 --> 01:01:08.850
but it won't go up if people
marry much younger women.

01:01:08.850 --> 01:01:10.340
That's one issue.

01:01:10.340 --> 01:01:18.170
Second issue, so in China, one
of things that people are often

01:01:18.170 --> 01:01:21.560
puzzled about is what's going
on with the savings rate?

01:01:24.750 --> 01:01:27.490
The savings rate in
China, personal savings,

01:01:27.490 --> 01:01:29.830
is historically unprecedented.

01:01:29.830 --> 01:01:33.740
People save 30%
of their incomes,

01:01:33.740 --> 01:01:35.400
as it is compared
to the US, which

01:01:35.400 --> 01:01:39.580
used to save zero till
recently, and after the crisis

01:01:39.580 --> 01:01:42.090
has gone up to 7%.

01:01:42.090 --> 01:01:46.300
So personal savings rates
in China are massive.

01:01:46.300 --> 01:01:49.540
So one puzzle people have
often been thinking about

01:01:49.540 --> 01:01:51.980
is what's going on with that?

01:01:51.980 --> 01:01:54.170
There's a very nice paper
which does the following.

01:01:56.680 --> 01:02:03.110
If I look at areas which have
high gender ratios, where

01:02:03.110 --> 01:02:08.380
women are really
scarce, are people

01:02:08.380 --> 01:02:11.478
saving more in those areas?

01:02:11.478 --> 01:02:12.520
Why would they save more?

01:02:17.310 --> 01:02:22.270
Why would you save more
if you're a parent of boy?

01:02:22.270 --> 01:02:25.370
So he particularly asked,
if you're a parent of a boy,

01:02:25.370 --> 01:02:27.800
in China it's easy, because
people have one child.

01:02:27.800 --> 01:02:30.133
So they're either a parent
of a boy or parent of a girl.

01:02:30.133 --> 01:02:34.220
If you're parent of a boy in an
area where the gender ratio is

01:02:34.220 --> 01:02:38.570
particularly bad,
so very few girls,

01:02:38.570 --> 01:02:40.700
it turns out you save more.

01:02:40.700 --> 01:02:41.850
Why would that be the case?

01:02:46.062 --> 01:02:47.470
AUDIENCE: The price of a bride.

01:02:47.470 --> 01:02:50.350
PROFESSOR: Yeah, you have
to buy your son a bride.

01:02:50.350 --> 01:02:52.160
So people are saving more.

01:02:52.160 --> 01:02:54.350
So basically they
suggest that people

01:02:54.350 --> 01:02:57.350
are beginning to
save more in order

01:02:57.350 --> 01:03:00.760
to buy their child a bride.

01:03:00.760 --> 01:03:03.230
And the way you buy it is
you don't go and buy her

01:03:03.230 --> 01:03:03.960
in the market.

01:03:03.960 --> 01:03:07.430
But basically women have become
very picky about not marrying

01:03:07.430 --> 01:03:09.280
into families,
which can't afford

01:03:09.280 --> 01:03:14.880
to give their son a
separate apartment to move

01:03:14.880 --> 01:03:18.380
into when they get married.

01:03:18.380 --> 01:03:20.450
China has very poor
mortgage markets.

01:03:20.450 --> 01:03:24.170
So you can't really take out
a mortgage to buy a house.

01:03:24.170 --> 01:03:26.670
So the only you buy a house is
you borrow from your parents.

01:03:26.670 --> 01:03:28.450
Your parents give you a gift.

01:03:28.450 --> 01:03:32.420
So parents now, it's become
a status issue for parents

01:03:32.420 --> 01:03:33.920
to marry this sons.

01:03:33.920 --> 01:03:37.190
And the way you marry your son
is by buying him an apartment

01:03:37.190 --> 01:03:38.640
when he gets married.

01:03:38.640 --> 01:03:40.430
So if you don't
have enough wealth,

01:03:40.430 --> 01:03:41.720
you can't buy an apartment.

01:03:41.720 --> 01:03:43.610
And then your son
can't get married.

01:03:43.610 --> 01:03:49.140
So this seems to be sort
of one of the facts that

01:03:49.140 --> 01:03:51.330
might actually
re-equilibriate China,

01:03:51.330 --> 01:03:53.180
because it seems
like one thing that's

01:03:53.180 --> 01:03:57.490
going on in China right now
is this massive increase

01:03:57.490 --> 01:03:58.035
in savings.

01:04:00.900 --> 01:04:03.130
The problem with this
method in increasing savings

01:04:03.130 --> 01:04:06.490
is it's a zero sum game.

01:04:06.490 --> 01:04:08.370
There are only so many girls.

01:04:08.370 --> 01:04:13.020
So the fact that I'm saving
a lot for my son just

01:04:13.020 --> 01:04:16.680
means my son can have a
bride, but not anybody else.

01:04:16.680 --> 01:04:19.880
It's not now that the number of
women go up because they save.

01:04:19.880 --> 01:04:23.150
So this is not really
solving the problem.

01:04:23.150 --> 01:04:25.920
It's squeezing parents.

01:04:25.920 --> 01:04:31.010
So if I were to save $1 more in
order to buy my son a house so

01:04:31.010 --> 01:04:34.290
he can get married,
then somebody else

01:04:34.290 --> 01:04:35.890
is going to want to
do the same thing.

01:04:35.890 --> 01:04:37.940
He's going to try to
save even more than me

01:04:37.940 --> 01:04:40.160
and buy a house for his son.

01:04:40.160 --> 01:04:44.880
So that competition is a very
kind of a negative competition.

01:04:44.880 --> 01:04:47.810
Everybody's getting hurt
by that competition.

01:04:47.810 --> 01:04:53.700
So that should make parents
really rethink this strategy.

01:04:53.700 --> 01:04:56.060
The one sign of hope
I see is this one,

01:04:56.060 --> 01:05:00.000
which is the market forces
seem to be kind of operating

01:05:00.000 --> 01:05:01.430
to make this problem better.

01:05:01.430 --> 01:05:06.160
That's true in China, much less
true in India, because India's

01:05:06.160 --> 01:05:07.230
population is growing.

01:05:07.230 --> 01:05:10.550
So there's still this
minimizing of adjustment.

01:05:10.550 --> 01:05:14.810
OK, so questions,
comments, before we stop?

01:05:14.810 --> 01:05:15.804
Yeah?

01:05:15.804 --> 01:05:20.058
AUDIENCE: In China, what you
see about the increase in girls

01:05:20.058 --> 01:05:22.016
again, because parents
see that they don't have

01:05:22.016 --> 01:05:23.260
to save money to have a girl?

01:05:23.260 --> 01:05:24.640
PROFESSOR: So you
might see that.

01:05:24.640 --> 01:05:26.310
You aren't seeing it yet.

01:05:26.310 --> 01:05:30.350
But maybe once it gets clear
that your daughter gets

01:05:30.350 --> 01:05:33.500
to live in a wonderful
apartment, et cetera,

01:05:33.500 --> 01:05:36.990
and having a son is a huge pain
because you have to now find

01:05:36.990 --> 01:05:40.290
ways to pay for
his apartment, you

01:05:40.290 --> 01:05:41.800
might start
preferring daughters.

01:05:41.800 --> 01:05:43.220
So maybe we'll see that.

01:05:49.180 --> 01:05:52.110
OK, thank you.

